# Held back: the CI header comment says OCR is gone.
#
# The branch bot has no `workflows` permission, so this one-comment change to
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` cannot be pushed from here and is held as a patch
# until someone with that permission applies it:
#
#     git apply docs/ci-workflow-comment.patch
#
# The change is a comment only - no job, step, matrix entry or command is
# touched, so CI behaves identically before and after. It is the header that
# still described the toolkit as deliberately free of any subtitle-provider
# mention: the suite's one networked code path (the exact-moviehash
# OpenSubtitles tier) is exercised against a fake transport, and the header now
# says so.
#
diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index f0728f5..f8dca3c 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -1,8 +1,9 @@
 # Continuous Integration — runs the entire offline test suite on every push
 # and pull request across all supported platforms and Python versions.
 #
-# The suite is deliberately hermetic: no media files, no mkvtoolnix, no ffprobe,
-# no OpenSubtitles API key, and no network access are required.
+# The suite is deliberately hermetic: no media files, no MKVToolNix, no FFmpeg,
+# no OpenSubtitles API key and no network access are required - the one
+# networked code path is tested against a fake transport.
 #
 # Hermetic means the suite does not *need* those things, not that it is blind to
 # them - a runner has none of them installed, so a test that behaves differently
